  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Saint-Tropez long had a reputation for being unwelcoming to dogs on its beaches. Under a municipal order, the town hall now allows dogs on leash on the town's beaches, but only at certain hours during the high season.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The rule, published on the town hall's official website, applies from 16 July to 15 September: dogs are allowed from 6am to 9.30am in the morning and from 6.30pm to 10pm in the evening, and banned the rest of the day during that period. Outside this summer window, access is more relaxed. The text asks owners to keep their dog on leash when approaching other beachgoers, to pick up after it (the town has installed poop-bag dispensers) and to respect the local flora and fauna. Guide dogs and working service dogs on duty remain allowed at all times.</p>
      <p>This text is a general town rule, worded for the "Saint-Tropez beaches" as a whole without listing each beach individually. It therefore presumably covers the town centre's public beaches: Bouillabaisse, Graniers, Canebiers and Salins.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: aim for the very first opening hours (6am-9.30am) to enjoy the sand before the crowds — the light is also at its best at that time in summer.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Saint-Tropez's historic centre, the harbour and the Place des Lices (with its Provençal market) are a few minutes away on foot or by car depending on the beach.</p>
    </div>

